🧠 E. Conceptual Questions (Koch-Focused)
Question	Answer
What is the difference between REST API and WebSockets?	
  REST is stateless and request–response based. WebSocket (used via Socket.IO) maintains a continuous connection for real-time communication.
  
How does JWT authentication work internally?
  A JWT has a header, payload, and signature. The server signs the token with a secret; when received later, the token is verified using the same secret.

What’s the difference between socket.emit and io.emit?	
  socket.emit() sends to one client, while io.emit() broadcasts to all connected clients.
  
What are HTTP-only cookies and why use them?	
  They are cookies that JavaScript cannot access — used for securely storing tokens and preventing XSS attacks.
  
What is event-driven programming?
  Code reacts to events like connections or messages instead of running sequentially — ideal for real-time systems.
  
How does MongoDB store data internally?	
  Data is stored as BSON (Binary JSON) documents inside collections — efficient for nested, flexible data.
  
What is CORS and why is it needed?	
  Cross-Origin Resource Sharing allows a frontend hosted on one domain to securely access a backend hosted elsewhere.
  
What happens when multiple users connect simultaneously?	
Each gets a unique socket ID; the server manages connections using events without blocking others (non-blocking I/O).
  
How do you ensure scalability for real-time apps?	
  Use socket rooms, Redis adapter for load balancing, horizontal scaling, and cloud-hosted DBs.
  
What improvements would you make?	
  Add group chats, message read receipts, typing indicators, notifications, and use Redis for socket clustering.


